An Ingress Controller acts as the single reverse proxy / layer 7 load balancer into your cluster, 
terminating HTTP requests and routing them based on hostnames (myapp.local) and URL paths without
requiring kubectl port-forward.

1. Enable NGINX Ingress Controller
If using Minikube:

minikube addons enable ingress

(Wait until ingress-nginx-controller pod in namespace ingress-nginx is Running):

kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx -w

2. Ingress Manifest (04-ingress.yaml)

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: frontend-ingress
  namespace: mini-project-1
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - host: myapp.local
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: frontend-svc
                port:
                  number: 80


Deploy it:

kubectl apply -f 04-ingress.yaml

2. Map myapp.local in /etc/hosts

Find your Minikube IP:

minikube ip

(Suppose the output is 192.168.49.2).

Add this IP to your host/WSL /etc/hosts file (requires sudo):

echo "$(minikube ip) myapp.local" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts

(On Windows host directly, add 127.0.0.1 myapp.local or the Minikube IP to C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts
 if accessing via Windows browser).

 3. Test Routing

curl http://myapp.local
Or open [http://myapp.local](http://myapp.local) in your browser. You should see the podinfo UI routing 
directly through the Ingress controller without any kubectl port-forward.


